That error means `data.users` is `undefined`, so `.map()` has nothing to run on. Check that the API returns a `users` array, then guard against missing or malformed data:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => {
    if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${r.status}`);
    return r.json();
  })
  .then((data) => {
    const users = Array.isArray(data?.users) ? data.users : [];
    render(users.map((u) => u.name));
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Could not load users:', error);
  });
```

The guard stops the crash; it won’t fix a mismatched API response shape, so inspect the response if you expected users to be there. The API managed to hide the important part in plain sight.

The response shape explains it: the array is at `data.data.users`, not `data.users`. My earlier guard would avoid the crash, but it would quietly render an empty list for this response. Use the correct path:

```js
fetch('/api/users')
  .then((r) => {
    if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`Request failed: ${r.status}`);
    return r.json();
  })
  .then((response) => {
    const users = response.data.users;
    render(users.map((u) => u.name));
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Could not load users:', error);
  });
```

If the API may return different shapes, validate that `response.data.users` is an array before mapping.

